WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
In Sidney Prescott’s room, next to her&nbsp;
desk, there is a poster for Swamp Ophelia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:07.680
an album by The Indigo Girls, a folk-rock band&nbsp;
from the 1980s. As you can see on the poster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.920
this band is a 2-piece. The&nbsp;
latest single from that album&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.800
at the time Scream came out was&nbsp;
also called “The Power of Two.”

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:16.720
This is symbolic because of the twist in Scream:&nbsp;
the fact that there are two killers. The power of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:20.480
two is the reason that they were able to trick&nbsp;
police and clear Billy’s name as a suspect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:23.680
With this in mind, we can go back and&nbsp;
find many more clues about Scream’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.200
double trouble antagonists. To learn the&nbsp;
connection between Stu Macher’s room and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.840
the video rental store, stick&nbsp;
around to the end of this video.

00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:35.106
(METAL INTRO)
(you should know what goes here by now if you watch my vids)

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:39.520
Everybody knows that Scream is a satire,&nbsp;
a horror movie that makes a commentary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.440
about horror movies. But I think a lot of&nbsp;
people overlook what a satire actually is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.760
So let’s take it back to the basic definition.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:51.040
Satire is the use of humor, irony, exaggeration,&nbsp;
or ridicule to expose and criticize something. By&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:55.920
that definition, satire has always been around,&nbsp;
long before Scream and long before movies. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.920
the slasher genre had already come out with&nbsp;
films like Student Bodies or Evil Dead 2.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:03.280
So let's just drop the charade that Scream&nbsp;
is some kind of first of it's kind movie.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.800
What one of the things that makes Scream&nbsp;
loved by many is its ability to satirize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.840
without turning to comedy, which led&nbsp;
to it being a legitimate force in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:13.280
genre that it was aiming to expose.&nbsp;
In fact, Scream was so successful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.360
and so many tried to imitate it, that it’s acute&nbsp;
self-awareness became the norm for a while.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.120
Audiences had seen this happen in television&nbsp;
a few years earlier with The Simpsons, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:25.040
family sitcom which attacked the positive family&nbsp;
values of other sitcoms on the air at the time.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.120
– [Lisa] Wow, it must be expensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.960
to produce all these cartoons.
– [Producer] Well, we cut corners. Sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:35.760
to save money, our animators will reuse the&nbsp;
same backgrounds over and over and over again.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:39.520
– As a result, everybody wanted to make a show&nbsp;
with that cutting edge social commentary like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:44.240
The Simpsons. But the Simpsons was a comedy; a&nbsp;
comedy that made fun of the TV families of 80s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:49.120
then surpassed them and became the TV family of&nbsp;
the 90s. Scream was able to follow this blueprint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:53.120
but for horror movies. You can make the argument&nbsp;
that Scream is funny, but it’s a different kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:58.000
of funny; it’s pretty clear the intention is not&nbsp;
to have you – as they put it in the 90s – ROFL.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.280
That isn’t to say screenwriter Kevin&nbsp;
Williamson and director Wes Craven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.560
don’t understand the formula of setup&nbsp;
and punchline. They actually transform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.840
that concept and use it for some of the&nbsp;
scares in the movie instead of laughs.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.760
For example, there are a number of scenes that end&nbsp;
with a big push-in to a spot in the background,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.680
where Ghostface can then be seen lurking. We see&nbsp;
this outside of Sid’s house, and at the grocery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:19.200
store, so we start to associate that particular&nbsp;
camera movement with the appearance of our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.440
antagonist. When Tatum is getting beers from the&nbsp;
garage, we see the camera brush past her and focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.680
on the doorframe. It starts to elevate our heart&nbsp;
rates, but then, nothing happens. That’s such a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.480
clever way to build suspense. Then we see the door&nbsp;
close, but it’s just the cat, so we’re no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.200
on edge, making it all the more effective when&nbsp;
Ghostface does appear in front of the closed door.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:40.080
There are other techniques like&nbsp;
this used to throw off the audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:44.415
and of course some clever easter eggs from Wes&nbsp;
Craven, so let’s get into the Things You Missed.

00:02:44.725 --> 00:02:49.179
(oooooohh SPOOKY MUSIC omg)

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.720
Even the title, Scream, feels&nbsp;
like it’s poking fun at some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:56.640
of the so-simple-it-could-be-about-anything&nbsp;
titles of Alfred Hitchcock’s horror movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:03.360
like Blackmail! Murder! Sabotage! Rope! And of&nbsp;
course, Psycho! The beginning of Scream pulls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:08.160
out a trick that was used in Psycho. Psycho came&nbsp;
out in 1960 and it’s biggest star was it’s female&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:12.160
lead Janet Leigh, who played Marion Crane.&nbsp;
She is prominently featured on the poster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:15.920
so audiences were totally shocked when they&nbsp;
killed her off very early on in the movie.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.840
Scream’s biggest star is Drew Barrymore, who is&nbsp;
similarly the face of the marketing campaign,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:23.360
so many people were shocked to see her&nbsp;
brutally disemboweled in the opening scene.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:26.240
There’s kind of a lineage that exists&nbsp;
connecting the most prominent slasher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.000
movie of each generation. When I covered Halloween&nbsp;
here on Things You Missed, I spent a lot of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.560
talking about how it was inspired by Psycho. Now&nbsp;
the torch is being passed down again to Scream.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:39.760
We can see this in the name: Loomis. Psycho had&nbsp;
Samuel Loomis, Halloween had Dr. Sam Loomis, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:44.628
Scream had Billy Loomis, who, after revealing he&nbsp;
is one of the killers, drops a quote from Psycho.

00:03:45.707 --> 00:03:48.660
– We all go a little mad sometimes.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:52.960
– Billy is also most likely the one on the phone&nbsp;
who asks Casey to guess his favorite movie.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:57.520
– [Casey] A Nightmare on Elm Street!
– [Caller] I like that movie, it was scary.&nbsp;

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:00.000
– [Casey] Well the first one&nbsp;
was, but the rest sucked.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.680
I was about to get up here and say that this&nbsp;
was a joke by director Wes Craven, who also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:07.120
directed the first Nightmare on Elm Street,&nbsp;
but not the five sequels that would follow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:11.040
But apparently it was in the script before it was&nbsp;
ever decided that Craven would be the director.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:15.120
– [Craven] I kinda wanted to take that&nbsp;
out, I did not write that line.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:19.040
– That answers my other question about that line.&nbsp;
Wes returned to the franchise to direct Wes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:22.720
Craven’s New Nightmare, in case you didn’t know&nbsp;
who the director of Wes Craven’s New Nightmare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:27.120
was… it was Wes Craven, which was the most recent&nbsp;
Freddy Krueger movie at the time Scream was made.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.840
It just seemed strange that he would&nbsp;
diss himself like that. Apparently the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:33.280
line later on, where Tatum mixes him up&nbsp;
with Halloween director John Carpenter…

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:36.960
– Don't go there, Sid. You’re starting to sound&nbsp;
like some Wes Carpenter flick or something.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:40.640
– [Zac] …was also in the script before Craven’s&nbsp;
involvement, but it kind of works better now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:45.040
seeing as how Scream uses Halloween as a template&nbsp;
for the horror movie tropes that it satirizes.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:49.040
After a few aggressive anonymous calls, Casey&nbsp;
immediately picks up the phone and yells…

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:51.040
– Listen ***hole!

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:55.280
– What if that had been someone else calling just&nbsp;
then? That would have been very rude Casey!

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:58.560
After breaking into the house, we see Ghostface&nbsp;
looking around for her. My question is:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:01.840
why doesn’t he just try calling the phone again&nbsp;
and going towards the sound of the ringtone?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:04.560
It seems logical that she would keep&nbsp;
the phone on her to try to get help,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:06.320
so she might have forgotten to silence it.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:07.840
After Ghostface catches up with her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:11.143
there are a couple of easter eggs that&nbsp;
play out on the Beckers’ side lawn.

00:05:11.394 --> 00:05:15.894
(cinematic impact)

00:05:16.496 --> 00:05:18.496
(industrial music)
(HOT BEAT!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!)

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:23.120
As I mentioned, Halloween is the&nbsp;
main template that Scream uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:26.160
for it’s satire. It’s the movie that Casey&nbsp;
is planning to watch in the opening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:29.440
and it’s the movie that crushes Stu’s head&nbsp;
in the finale. There’s another Halloween&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:33.360
connection after Casey manages to get outside&nbsp;
and sees her parents going back to the house.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:37.840
Despite the title of this movie, she is unable&nbsp;
to find her scream and get her parents attention.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.080
– (barely audible) Mom…

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:42.240
– In Halloween II, we see this same phenomenon take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:45.600
place as Laurie Strode tries to yell for&nbsp;
help outside of Haddonfield Hospital.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:50.000
– (barely audible) Help… help...

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:52.840
– Then there’s yet another Halloween&nbsp;
reference just moments later.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:58.000
– Get in the car. Drive down to&nbsp;
the MacKenzies’. Call the police.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.440
– When Laurie and the kids are targeted by&nbsp;
Michael Myers, she gives them a similar order.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.360
– I want you to go down the&nbsp;
street to the MacKenzies'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:05.680
house. I want you to tell them to call the police.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:09.360
– I’m sure there will be a lot of people that&nbsp;
already know about that easter egg. And honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:13.360
quite a few of the eggs in this movie are pretty&nbsp;
common knowledge because of the nature of Scream;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.840
it’s basically a movie that’s so&nbsp;
loaded with references and nods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:18.320
that it’s going to attract people&nbsp;
who already know about that stuff.

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:22.240
On Things You Missed™, I try to stick to things&nbsp;
you might have missed, that’s why it’s called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:25.840
that, if you didn’t know. However, if I leave&nbsp;
out the ones that everyone knows about then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:29.120
there are gonna be people in the comments&nbsp;
complaining that I missed stuff. So let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:32.400
just go through the ones that I think are more&nbsp;
obvious real quick and get them out of the way.

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:35.600
Billy climbs through Sidney's window and&nbsp;
startles her, just like Glen did to Nancy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:38.880
in A Nightmare on Elm Street. Sheriff Burke&nbsp;
is played by the actor who plays the police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:43.200
officer in Nightmare on Elm St. Tatum’s number&nbsp;
10 crop top is very similar to a shirt worn by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:47.840
Glen in A Nightmare on Elm St. Principal Himbry&nbsp;
has the following conversation with the janitor.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:50.080
– [Himbry] Damn little sh*ts
– [Janitor] What’d you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:53.280
call me?
– Not you Fred.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:56.320
– [Zac] That’s director Wes Craven cameoing&nbsp;
as Fred, which is short for Freddy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:59.200
because he’s dressed up like Freddy&nbsp;
Krueger from A Nightmare On Elm Street.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:00.880
– [Rizzo] Frederick! Freddie!

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:04.080
When Ghostface pins down Sidney, he&nbsp;
cocks his head just like Michael Myers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:08.560
and the movie makes a point to show that scene&nbsp;
during Stu’s party. Sidney refers to the town of Woodsboro&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:11.920
under curfew as The Town That Dreaded&nbsp;
Sundown. And Randy, who yells at the TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:15.840
to warn Jamie Lee Curtis about the threat&nbsp;
behind her, also has a threat behind him.

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:19.360
– [Randy] Jamie, look behind you. Look behind you.

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:21.440
– The actor that plays Randy is also named Jamie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:24.640
and he’s being watched in a similar&nbsp;
manner by Kenny, the news camera guy.

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:26.800
And speaking of people named Jamie, Sidney at one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:30.080
point escapes from the attic out onto&nbsp;
the roof like Jamie from Halloween 4.

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:32.160
I think those are all the&nbsp;
more well known easter eggs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:34.640
so let's get into the fun&nbsp;
stuff: the less obvious ones.

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:38.240
When Billy sneaks into Sid’s room, he tells&nbsp;
her he was watching the Exorcist on TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:41.680
with all the good parts cut out, and it made him&nbsp;
think of their relationship. From this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:44.320
they continue to reference back to&nbsp;
this idea that their relationship is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:47.600
like a movie. After Sid’s close call&nbsp;
with Ghostface, a reporter asks her…

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:50.251
– So how does it feel to be&nbsp;
almost brutally butchered?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:53.200
People want to know, they have a right to know.

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:58.000
– And this is a cameo by Linda Blair, she played Regan in The&nbsp;
Exorcist 23 years earlier. If you’re listening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:00.880
closely during this Sidney and Billy scene,&nbsp;
you’ll notice that there is a song playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:04.800
in the background. It’s an acoustic cover of&nbsp;
Don’t Fear The Reaper by Blue Oyster Cult.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:09.120
A song that I like, but I am starting to get a&nbsp;
little tired of hearing in movies and TV. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:12.800
I’ll save that rant for a future video, because&nbsp;
here, there might be a good reason for it:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:16.800
it’s the same song that Laurie Strode and Annie&nbsp;
Brackett listen to in the car in Halloween. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:20.480
as I’ve said many times already, Halloween&nbsp;
is the template that Scream builds off of.

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:24.080
There could also be a second meaning here, the&nbsp;
Ghostface costume is essentially a depiction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:27.600
a grim reaper. The name of the costume&nbsp;
itself in universe is “Father Death.”

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:31.920
The next day the school is buzzing with rumors&nbsp;
and news crews trying to capitalize on the story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:35.440
We first see one of our main characters, Gail&nbsp;
Weathers, in the background of an establishing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:39.280
shot. Even though we don’t end up going towards&nbsp;
her here, the neon green outfit in a sea of dull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:43.200
wardrobe colors draws the eye and lets us know&nbsp;
she’s important. It’s like in a video game where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:47.120
it will only let you target characters that you&nbsp;
can actually talk to. That’s good filmmaking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:51.440
It’s also kind of a preview of how the movie&nbsp;
ends, but Gail has a bloody lip and a red dress.

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:55.040
During school that day, each student is called&nbsp;
into the office for interrogation. There’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:58.720
chart on the wall that details which teachers&nbsp;
are in which classrooms each period. Most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:02.640
the teachers’ names are crew reference easter&nbsp;
eggs. Almost all of these refer to people in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:06.080
the art department, who would have been&nbsp;
the ones to put these decorations on set.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:09.600
They include carpenter Art Williams,&nbsp;
local lead person Kenny Montante,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:13.920
additional set decorator Debra Echard, leadman&nbsp;
Richard Lambert, set dresser Josh Elliot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:18.160
art department production assistant Charlene&nbsp;
Bar, art director David Lubin, driver Ben Davis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:22.560
and set decorator Michele Poulik, probably a&nbsp;
very unfortunate name to have to grow up with.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:26.080
We actually see Michele put her name into&nbsp;
the movie a couple times. In the hallway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:30.320
there’s a poster advertising Michelle Palik for&nbsp;
President, a deliberate misspelling of her name.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:33.360
In the bathroom, there’s some graffiti&nbsp;
that says “Michele + Bob 4 ever”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:36.160
using the same unconventional&nbsp;
spelling as the set decorator.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:38.640
Because of her situation with her&nbsp;
mom being killed a year prior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:40.720
the media attention is very familiar for Sid.

00:09:41.282 --> 00:09:44.800
– [Sidney] You know the police and reporters and&nbsp;
everything, it’s like Deja Vu all over again.

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:48.400
– That’s a redundant statement. That would be&nbsp;
like saying I went on a side-tangent. Or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:51.280
if I commented on Scream’s acute self-awareness.

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:54.400
One of the things Scream does to&nbsp;
stay interesting is play with our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:58.717
expectations. Pay attention to the music sting&nbsp;
that happens when Sidney opens the closet door.

00:10:01.185 --> 00:10:03.185
(ghostly whisper in music)

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:09.920
Using typical horror movie logic, the audience&nbsp;
can expect that this means a scare is about to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:14.000
happen. It’s the perfect setup for that classic&nbsp;
refrigerator door scare, but when she closes it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:18.000
nobody is there. This also establishes&nbsp;
the idea that the closet should be empty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:21.040
making it even better when Ghostface&nbsp;
busts out of it 5 minutes later.

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:23.040
– [Ghostface] With the murders&nbsp;
and all, it’s like 

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:25.280
right out of a horror movie or something.

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:27.440
– We see more of that aforementioned self-awareness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:29.920
as Sidney mocks horror movies,&nbsp;
saying they are all the same.

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:33.600
– …some stupid killer stalking some big&nbsp;
breasted girl who can’t act, who’s always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:36.480
running up the stairs when she should be&nbsp;
going out the front door, it’s insulting.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:40.080
– …only for her to do just that when she can’t&nbsp;
figure out how to unlock the front door.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:44.320
I always love this next part. She calls the police&nbsp;
on her PC, connects to someone seconds later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:47.760
and the cops are at the door in&nbsp;
46 seconds. Take notes Uber Eats.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:52.000
So Sidney is saved by the time Tatum&nbsp;
shows up in her red VW Beetle. I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:55.280
know 100% if this is an intentional&nbsp;
reference, but since it’s Scream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:58.400
I’ll just assume that it is. This is&nbsp;
the car that Jack Torrance drives in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:02.080
the novel version of The Shining. It appears&nbsp;
in Stanley Kubrick’s film adaptation as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:06.000
as the car that’s been crushed by an overturned&nbsp;
truck, Kubrick’s way of saying, screw you King,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:09.760
I’m not gonna follow the book. Of course, there&nbsp;
is a more obvious Stephen King reference later on.

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:14.080
– Corn syrup, same stuff they&nbsp;
use for pig’s blood in Carrie.

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:18.240
– Tatum also goes with to the police station, where&nbsp;
they get some info from Sidney. Her brother Dewey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:21.840
tries to get her to respect him while he’s&nbsp;
in uniform in front of his superior officer.

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:25.280
– [Dewey] He’s my superior.
– [Tatum] The janitor’s your superior.

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:29.360
– Which is true… on the film set,&nbsp;
since Wes Craven, the director,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:32.400
plays the janitor. Technically&nbsp;
he’s David Arquette’s boss.

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:34.800
Billy’s name is cleared&nbsp;
when Stu calls Sidney again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:38.560
using the Ghostface voice while Billy is&nbsp;
still in custody. Sheriff Burke is no L.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:40.960
I don’t even know if he’s on the&nbsp;
level of Randy, to be honest.

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:43.120
– He’s got killer printed all over his forehead.

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:46.000
– Which is exactly where Sidney&nbsp;
shoots him at the end of the movie.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:49.520
Randy is portrayed as the biggest buff&nbsp;
in the story. He works at the video store,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:53.200
and knows all the rules about horror movies.&nbsp;
We see these rules referenced time and time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:56.600
again by the killer on the phone, and that&nbsp;
leads us to see Randy as a prime suspect.

00:11:56.600 --> 00:12:00.240
– I’m the first to admit it. If this were&nbsp;
a scary movie, I’d be the prime suspect.

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:02.960
– However, there may be someone&nbsp;
who is an equally big movie buff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:06.160
and that is Stu Macher. In fact, there&nbsp;
are a couple clues that link him to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:10.080
video store just as much as Randy. The store&nbsp;
has these two posters on display. The one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:14.560
on the left is a poster for the 1993 film&nbsp;
Mother’s Boys, starring Jamie Lee Curtis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:18.720
Stu is a big fan of Jamie Lee. All the&nbsp;
videos he rents for his party have her in them.

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:22.400
– Terror train. Prom night.&nbsp;
How come Jamie Lee Curtis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:26.816
is in all of these movies?
– She’s the Scream Queen.&nbsp;

00:12:26.816 --> 00:12:29.600
– [Stu] And with a set of lungs&nbsp;
like that, she should be.

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:34.000
– On the right is a poster for the band Greenjelly.&nbsp;
This same poster can be seen when Sidney runs into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:38.480
Stu’s room to escape Ghostface, so I think&nbsp;
it just serves as a link to show us that Stu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:41.360
probably spends more time than&nbsp;
we’d expect at the video store.

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:44.400
The scene of everyone closing up&nbsp;
shop in town is just ruined for me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:47.280
Twenty-twenty has ruined a lot of&nbsp;
movies. I just imagine in real life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:52.880
there would be retards protesting on the streets;&nbsp;defying the curfew and 
saying things like: "there is no killer, it’s all a hoax."

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:56.640
At this point in the movie, there are a few&nbsp;
innuendos as well. Tatum tells Sidney that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.640 --> 00:13:00.240
her intimacy issues will thaw out with&nbsp;
time as she removes a case of popsicles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:03.920
from the freezer. This basically tells us&nbsp;
that Sidney's getting ready to lose her virginity.

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:08.720
Then there’s this… meme-worthy moment. Our eyes&nbsp;
are drawn to a certain part of the frame, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:13.600
of course is Tatum’s lungs. Then our eyes are&nbsp;
drawn to another part of the frame. This spiral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:18.000
pattern just kind of draws you in, it’s like a&nbsp;
hypnosis. Even though we don’t see her having sex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:22.000
we know that Tatum falls victim to rule number&nbsp;
one, because her death occurs when she gets stuck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:26.560
in the pet door in the garage, and she’s unable to&nbsp;
get out because her tits and cheeks are too big.

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:29.760
Also the song playing when she enters&nbsp;
the garage is "Drop Dead Gorgeous" by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:34.080
Republica. Scream was doing death omen songs&nbsp;
four years before Final Destination came out.

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:36.640
That night, Sidney does end up&nbsp;
giving up the v-card to Billy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:40.880
and Ghostface breaks in and appears to kill him,&nbsp;
but obviously we know it was just corn syrup.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:44.480
The was Stu cleans up off the knife is&nbsp;
interesting to me. The motion almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:49.120
kind of resembles taking off a condom, and for&nbsp;
Sidney, it comes immediately as a post-sex act.

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:51.280
And Stu later admits that he&nbsp;
did have a thing for Sidney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:54.000
which she responds to by pushing&nbsp;
the TV onto him and saying

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:55.643
– In your dreams.

00:13:55.957 --> 00:13:57.440
(Stu SCREAMing; source of movie title)

00:13:57.440 --> 00:14:00.880
– It’s kind of a karma moment, because Stu&nbsp;
was the one to laugh in Randy’s face when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:02.720
he asked if he might have a chance with Sidney.

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:04.000
After everything we’ve been through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:07.558
it’s only fitting that the action&nbsp;
ends with one last meta-horror trope.

00:14:08.519 --> 00:14:11.600
– [Randy] This is the moment when the&nbsp;
supposedly dead killer comes back to life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:14.414
For one last scare.
– Augh!&nbsp;

00:14:14.414 --> 00:14:15.315
(screams)
(gunshot)

00:14:15.936 --> 00:14:17.200
– Not in my movie.

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:19.520
– What do you mean not in my movie?&nbsp;
It literally just happened.

00:14:19.520 --> 00:14:23.201
Before we can end this video, there is&nbsp;
one more thing I want to talk about.

00:14:23.201 --> 00:14:25.361
(cinematic impact)

00:14:28.135 --> 00:14:32.000
(hot AND I MEAN HOT spooky dance music)

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:35.680
The big surprise in Scream, that again,&nbsp;
like the camerawork and writing choices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:39.040
that I brought up in this video, uses&nbsp;
our horror-genre expectations against us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:43.120
is the fact that there are two killers instead of&nbsp;
the usual one that we get in most slasher movies.

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:46.960
The clues were there all along. In the Casey&nbsp;
Becker scene, one Ghostface breaks into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:50.720
house and runs straight to the front door. We&nbsp;
don’t know why at the time, but it was to let his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:55.120
accomplice inside. You can actually spot that the&nbsp;
door is now open when Casey’s parents get home.

00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:57.840
At the fountain, Stu explains&nbsp;
to Sidney how you gut someone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.840 --> 00:14:59.760
Billy makes eye contact with him and says,

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:02.640
– Hey, it’s called tact you f*ckrag.

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:04.560
– We assume that he’s standing up for his girlfriend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:08.640
but he might actually be trying to shut Stu up&nbsp;
before he says too much and gives them away.

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:12.320
This might be obvious, but Sidney first&nbsp;
suspects Billy when she sees the cell phone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:16.480
fall out of his pocket. If he has a cell phone,&nbsp;
why does he need to come sneak through the window to talk to her?

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:19.920
Then there are multiple instances&nbsp;
of Billy just acting sus. Like when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:23.680
Sidney admits that she doesn’t think he’s&nbsp;
guilty, he keeps trying to convince her.

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:28.400
– You still think it’s me?
– No I don’t, it’s just somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:33.040
was there, Billy. Someone tried to kill me.
– I know, I know, the cops said I scared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:37.040
him away. It wasn’t me Sid.
– I know. He called me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:41.120
again last night at Tatum’s house.
– See? Couldn’t have been me, I was in jail.

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:43.120
– And this is obviously him trying too hard to drive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:45.680
home the point that the second&nbsp;
Ghostface call exonerates him.

00:15:45.680 --> 00:15:47.280
Stu does a similar thing at the fountain.

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:50.677
– [Stu] No, I didn’t kill anybody.
– [Billy] Nobody said you did.

00:15:50.960 --> 00:15:54.400
– [Zac] Then at the school, there are a couple of&nbsp;
pranksters who run around in Ghostface masks, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:58.240
they find themselves sitting in the principal’s&nbsp;
office. One is wearing blue flannel with a white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:01.840
t-shirt underneath, which is what Billy wears&nbsp;
to the party, and the other wearing a muted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:06.080
long sleeve shirt, not the same color, but very&nbsp;
similar in style to what Stu has on at the party.

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:09.280
Now I want you to look very closely, right here…

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:11.520
(jumpscare)

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:15.360
Had to get that one last scare in. I’ll be&nbsp;
analyzing all four Screams and maybe the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:19.120
fifth once they release it, if they ever get&nbsp;
around to that. Look for my Scream playlist on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:23.440
the left and remember to subscribe to CZsWorld&nbsp;
for new horrors every week, ring the deathbell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.440 --> 00:16:28.359
for all notifications, and I’ll see you in&nbsp;
the next video. Assuming we both survive.

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:30.620
(spooky yet funky-fresh dance music)

